    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup for LawDog hinges on capitalizing on favorable platoon splits and high-value spots in the batting order. **Cal Raleigh** (72.48) is our premier play, benefiting from a soft matchup against Gage Jump and a significant park factor boost that drives his efficiency well above the baseline. Similarly, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (69.94) remains an anchor, providing a elite floor even against a competent arm in Prielipp, while **Bryce Harper** (65.58) and **Jung Hoo Lee** (64.27) anchor the outfield. **Jung Hoo Lee** is particularly intriguing; despite the elite hold of the opposing pitcher, a favorable wind out and a strong platoon advantage elevate his output. **Kyle Karros** (63.83) rounds out our top-tier starters, as his matchup against Sears offers both a high SP_xERA and a potent platoon edge that pushes him into the \"Strong\" start category.\n\nOn the bench, the decision to sit **Dylan Beavers** (49.94) was the most difficult. Despite his premium slot at the top of the order providing a +10% boost, his efficiency was ultimately capped by a difficult matchup against Miles and the superior overall projection of players like **Zack Gelof** and **Lawrence Butler**. Speaking of **Lawrence Butler**, he carries the highest volatility on the roster today; while his park factor and platoon edge against Bryan Woo are excellent, his poor BvP metrics dragged his score down to 58.12. Managers should keep a close watch on **Yainer Diaz** (42.02), who faces a tough assignment against Ian Seymour; if his lineup position falls lower than our #5 assumption, he risks dipping into the \"Below floor\" tier, making him a primary candidate for a late-game swap if we need to preserve game caps.",
